WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:11.713
This is Chapter 4: attack surface
reduction, through audit, warn and block.

4
00:00:11.713 --> 00:00:14.226
Audit is configured but protects nothing.

5
00:00:14.226 --> 00:00:19.613
A rule is done when it's in block on every
ring, or its exception is written down.

6
00:00:19.613 --> 00:00:24.301
The code's job is to say which rules can
move this week, and why the others can't.

7
00:00:24.301 --> 00:00:28.437
We'll start from Learn's modes
and syntax, read the rule catalog,

8
00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:31.763
the plan and the readiness
rules, and walk two labs.

9
00:00:31.763 --> 00:00:34.630
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved;

10
00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:38.340
the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.865
Why does this rollout need code?

12
00:00:43.865 --> 00:00:46.646
Because it's fourteen
rules times five rings,

13
00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:49.727
each pair with its own clock
and its own audit hits.

14
00:00:49.727 --> 00:00:52.465
Audit is configured, but protects nothing.

15
00:00:52.465 --> 00:00:56.833
By the end of this chapter you can produce
the rule-by-ring matrix the assessor

16
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:59.632
reads, say which rules each
ring can move this week,

17
00:00:59.632 --> 00:01:02.840
and hand the change board the
exact value that moves them.

18
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.608
It's for the developers, the
platform owner who owns this gate,

19
00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:10.452
and the security operations lead
who reads the weekly counts.

20
00:01:10.452 --> 00:01:12.757
The rule list and the stage lengths,

21
00:01:12.757 --> 00:01:17.720
thirty days of audit and fourteen of
warn, are the Devices book's policy.

22
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:25.292
Microsoft Learn gives you
the rule modes: zero off,

23
00:01:25.292 --> 00:01:29.020
one block, two audit, five
not configured, six warn.

24
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:34.533
The custom-profile path in Intune, with
one rule GUID equals mode per line.

25
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:40.277
Warn's requirements: Windows 10 1809 or
later, two rules that don't support it,

26
00:01:40.277 --> 00:01:44.075
the LSASS rule and the
Office code-injection rule,

27
00:01:44.075 --> 00:01:49.633
and since platform 4.18.26060, Unblock
needs an administrator's approval.

28
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:54.482
The rules reference gives each rule's GUID
and its advanced hunting action types.

29
00:01:54.482 --> 00:01:57.639
And Learn lists a known
issue: on Windows Server,

30
00:01:57.639 --> 00:02:01.490
a rule can show as compliant
without being enforced.

31
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.577
What does that leave to you?

32
00:02:06.577 --> 00:02:09.502
Nothing ties an event back
to its rule and ring,

33
00:02:09.502 --> 00:02:13.427
so the kit carries the fourteen
rules with their action types.

34
00:02:13.427 --> 00:02:17.853
A count can't say whether a hit already
has an exclusion, so the per-device

35
00:02:17.853 --> 00:02:21.828
query keeps the file path, and each
hit is matched to the register.

36
00:02:21.828 --> 00:02:26.689
Someone has to count days, so the plan
records each stage and its start date.

37
00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:30.490
A plan might put a no-warn rule
in warn, so that's an error.

38
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:34.865
Fourteen lines per ring by hand
invites typos, so the kit writes them.

39
00:02:34.865 --> 00:02:39.503
And a compliance report can be wrong on
servers, so the device itself is checked.

40
00:02:39.503 --> 00:02:43.580
The verdict: keep Learn
exactly; build the bookkeeping.

41
00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:52.894
The ASR check reads the plan, the exclusion
register, the inventory and the device

42
00:02:52.894 --> 00:02:58.043
states, runs the guide's query and its
per-device form, and writes five things.

43
00:02:58.043 --> 00:03:03.130
The matrix: each rule's stage on each
ring, which is what the assessor reads.

44
00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:06.918
Readiness: per ring and
rule, the stage, days in it,

45
00:03:06.918 --> 00:03:12.843
audit hits and open hits, blocks, the
next stage, and a status with the reason.

46
00:03:12.843 --> 00:03:16.692
The custom-profile value
for each ring, now and next.

47
00:03:16.692 --> 00:03:18.968
What each device actually enforces.

48
00:03:18.968 --> 00:03:21.193
And the exclusions that have expired.

49
00:03:21.193 --> 00:03:24.278
A status is ready, hold, done, warn, fail,

50
00:03:24.278 --> 00:03:29.680
or not applicable for a rule
Windows Server doesn't support.

51
00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:35.068
Here are the readiness rules,

52
00:03:35.068 --> 00:03:38.480
which are the program's design
from the Devices book's policy.

53
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.100
A rule in audit is ready after thirty
days, when every audit hit on the

54
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.555
ring's devices has an exclusion, a
fix or a decision in the register.

55
00:03:47.555 --> 00:03:51.568
It moves to warn, or straight to
block if it doesn't support warn.

56
00:03:51.568 --> 00:03:55.555
A rule in warn is ready after
fourteen days with no open hit.

57
00:03:55.555 --> 00:04:00.171
A hit is covered by a valid register
row for its rule, or for every rule,

58
00:04:00.171 --> 00:04:03.880
whose path is the same file,
or a folder that contains it.

59
00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.121
And the change board, not the kit,

60
00:04:06.121 --> 00:04:10.110
puts the next value in
Intune, under a change record.

61
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:14.910
Who's involved?

62
00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.975
The platform owner owns this gate,
keeps the plan and the register,

63
00:04:18.975 --> 00:04:21.610
and brings ready rules
to the change board.

64
00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:25.503
The security operations lead
reads blocks per rule per week,

65
00:04:25.503 --> 00:04:29.085
and starts any new rule from
Microsoft at audit on ring 1.

66
00:04:29.085 --> 00:04:32.441
And the help desk is briefed
before each warn stage,

67
00:04:32.441 --> 00:04:35.397
because Unblock now
needs an administrator.

68
00:04:35.397 --> 00:04:39.522
One note: Microsoft's own position
is lighter than the program's.

69
00:04:39.522 --> 00:04:44.084
Learn says standard protection rules can
go to block without extensive testing.

70
00:04:44.084 --> 00:04:49.770
The program runs all three stages, and
the kit enforces the program's stages.

71
00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.620
Lab 1, in the kit's folder.

72
00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.783
Step 1 lists the rules
and which skip warn.

73
00:04:58.783 --> 00:05:00.557
Step 2 runs the check.

74
00:05:00.557 --> 00:05:02.020
Ring 0 is done.

75
00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.615
Ring 1 has twelve rules ready for block;

76
00:05:04.615 --> 00:05:08.832
the Office child-process rule is
held, six of fourteen warn days.

77
00:05:08.832 --> 00:05:12.656
Ring 2 is ready to leave audit,
except the same Office rule,

78
00:05:12.656 --> 00:05:14.933
with one audit hit nobody decided.

79
00:05:14.933 --> 00:05:17.890
The PSExec and WMI rule
is a warning there,

80
00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:21.632
because Configuration
Manager manages one device.

81
00:05:21.632 --> 00:05:27.183
Ring 3's plan puts the LSASS rule in warn,
which it doesn't support: a plan error.

82
00:05:27.183 --> 00:05:32.000
And one server enforces none of the
thirteen rules its plan puts in audit.

83
00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.775
Step 3 reads why the ring 2 rule is held.

84
00:05:38.775 --> 00:05:41.450
Seventeen audit events, one open hit.

85
00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:45.780
Two paths fired the rule: the
line-of-business helper, which has a per-rule

86
00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.394
exclusion in the register,

87
00:05:47.394 --> 00:05:51.063
and a macro runner in a public
tools folder, which has nothing.

88
00:05:51.063 --> 00:05:55.475
Someone decides an exclusion or a
fix before the rule moves to warn.

89
00:05:55.475 --> 00:05:58.587
Step 4 prints the value that moves ring 1.

90
00:05:58.587 --> 00:06:04.887
Every rule at one, block, except the Office
child-process rule, still at six, warn.

91
00:06:04.887 --> 00:06:09.580
That's the exact string the change
board pastes into the ring 1 policy.

92
00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.216
Lab 2 uses a test
tenant, never production,

93
00:06:16.216 --> 00:06:20.205
with the policy assigned to a
ring 0 device group in audit.

94
00:06:20.205 --> 00:06:24.442
Collect the test device's state,
sign in, and run the check live.

95
00:06:24.442 --> 00:06:28.167
The device check should show
every rule in audit on the device.

96
00:06:28.167 --> 00:06:32.193
A row saying not configured on the
device while the plan says audit

97
00:06:32.193 --> 00:06:36.292
is either the server issue, or a
policy the device hasn't received yet.

98
00:06:36.292 --> 00:06:39.730
Compare with Learn's own
one-line listing on the device.

99
00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:40.905
Nothing changed.

100
00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:44.808
If you set a rule locally for an
investigation, Learn's cmdlet puts

101
00:06:44.808 --> 00:06:49.220
it back in audit, and Intune overwrites
it at the next refresh anyway.

102
00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:56.295
Here's what this gate proves,
against NIST's catalog.

103
00:06:56.295 --> 00:06:58.214
Each rule's stage on each ring,

104
00:06:58.214 --> 00:07:01.733
with the date it began,
supports least functionality.

105
00:07:01.733 --> 00:07:07.382
The value each ring is set to, now and
next, supports configuration settings.

106
00:07:07.382 --> 00:07:10.571
What devices actually enforce,
with blocks per rule,

107
00:07:10.571 --> 00:07:12.870
supports malicious code protection.

108
00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.845
And exclusions with
owners and expiry dates,

109
00:07:15.845 --> 00:07:20.508
with the expired ones listed, supports
configuration settings again.

110
00:07:20.508 --> 00:07:23.695
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
as the guide maps them.

111
00:07:23.695 --> 00:07:27.880
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

112
00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.030
What breaks in production?

113
00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:36.284
A server that reports
compliant but enforces nothing;

114
00:07:36.284 --> 00:07:38.743
the device check reads the server itself.

115
00:07:38.743 --> 00:07:41.492
A rule moved on a
calendar, not on its hits,

116
00:07:41.492 --> 00:07:46.242
which breaks a line-of-business app;
every hit is dispositioned first.

117
00:07:46.242 --> 00:07:50.592
A no-warn rule in warn, which
acts as block on older Windows.

118
00:07:50.592 --> 00:07:55.255
A rule set in both Intune and Group
Policy: Group Policy wins by default,

119
00:07:55.255 --> 00:07:58.230
and the device check
shows the value that won.

120
00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:02.405
A folder exclusion that's too
wide, so file paths are preferred.

121
00:08:02.405 --> 00:08:05.984
And warn treated as a
bypass: brief the help desk,

122
00:08:05.984 --> 00:08:09.460
and turn repeated needs into exclusions.

123
00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:14.560
Here's what done looks like.

124
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.648
The plan matches each ring's Intune
policy, with the change record that set it.

125
00:08:19.648 --> 00:08:22.335
No rule without warn is planned in warn.

126
00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:27.697
Every audit hit has an exclusion, a fix
or a decision before its rule moves.

127
00:08:27.697 --> 00:08:30.722
Every server's device
check matches its plan.

128
00:08:30.722 --> 00:08:33.997
No expired exclusion
remains in the register.

129
00:08:33.997 --> 00:08:36.947
And the run folder is
recorded as evidence.

130
00:08:36.947 --> 00:08:40.423
Next, Chapter 5 turns from
settings to findings:

131
00:08:40.423 --> 00:08:43.898
who owns each exposed
device, and each incident.

132
00:08:43.898 --> 00:08:45.060
Thanks for watching.
